From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:38:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 01/16] luks2: LUKS2 volumes, their crypto, the luks command
 and host tests

The LUKS2 code (src/libs/luks2, headers/private/luks2), written from the
LUKS2 on-disk format specification and the RFCs, shared by the kernel
driver, the boot loader, the luks command and the build host:
- both header copies with their checksums, the JSON metadata (a small
  parser and serialiser that keeps unknown fields), one crypt segment with
  aes-xts-plain64 and 512 to 4096-byte sectors, luks2 keyslots with
  argon2id, argon2i and pbkdf2 (sha256, sha512), the luks1
  anti-forensic split and the pbkdf2 digest; interrupted reencryptions,
  integrity and detached headers are refused;
- creating volumes in cryptsetup's layout and adding or removing keyslots.

Crypto behind a small interface: Thomas Pornin's constant-time AES (MIT,
from compat/openbsd_wlan) with AES-NI as an optional x86_64 accelerator
(arch/x86, with an arm64 slot), XTS, SHA-256/512, HMAC, PBKDF2, BLAKE2b and
Argon2 (single-threaded, one allocation per lane). Byte order is explicit
everywhere.

luks (src/bin/luks): format, dump, test, addkey, removekey, read, write,
and on Haiku open, close and list through the luks_disk driver. It also
builds on Linux, for images and tests.

Tests (src/tests/system/luks2, run_tests.sh): FIPS-197, IEEE 1619, FIPS
180-4, RFC 7914, RFC 7693 and RFC 9106 vectors; random comparisons with
OpenSSL; 40 interoperability tests with cryptsetup 2.8.4 in both
directions, data byte for byte; with and without AddressSanitizer.

The crypto code is unreviewed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
